      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait so i need to change the resource line to \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*\" to include the objects? i'll try that now. \n\nanyway just to be safe, if the resource is fixed but i still get errors, what else could be \"guarding\" the bucket? like is there any other way access gets blocked even if the IAM policy is perfect?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're very close. Yes, changing the resource to `arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*` will allow uploads to all objects within that bucket.\n\nAs for your question, yes, there are other ways access can be blocked even if the IAM policy is perfect. Here are a couple:\n\n- A bucket policy (also separate from the IAM policy) that explicitly denies objects.\n- A governance policy set at the AWS organization level that might block certain actions.\n- The Region itself might have a denied procedure for specific actions.\n- Temporary securityTokens might need to be passed to the role anyway, and this part of the authentication flow might still be refusing the permission.\n\nBut in general, it's worth checking the most basic ones.",
